<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <RootNamespace>CupriLex.Compiler</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>CupriLex.Compiler</AssemblyName>
    <InvariantGlobalization>true</InvariantGlobalization>

    <!--
      A library, and the only thing in this repository that is published. The surveys that walk
      the corpus live in cli/ so that a consumer gets the translation and nothing else: an alpha
      that shipped Reader and Emit as public types would have somebody compiling against them by
      accident, and after that they could not move.
    -->
    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
    <PackageId>CupriLex.Compiler</PackageId>
    <Version>0.1.0-alpha.1</Version>
    <Authors>Wixely</Authors>
    <Description>Browser HTML in, CupriFace-safe HTML out, and a report of everything that could not come with it. Compiles GSAP timelines to static CSS keyframes, packages a composition as a .cutpkg, and lists every request a document would make off the machine before making any of them.</Description>
    <PackageTags>cupriface;cupricut;gsap;html;css;animation</PackageTags>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>MIT</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/Wixely/CupriLex</PackageProjectUrl>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/Wixely/CupriLex</RepositoryUrl>
    <RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>

    <!--
      The XML comments here ARE the documentation, and both of these warnings would make them
      worse. CS1591 demands one on every public member of a package whose whole point is that most
      of it is not public. CS1573 demands one per PARAMETER as soon as any parameter is documented,
      which turns a summary that explains the one field worth explaining into a wall of restated
      names. A filler line is not documentation.
    -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS1591;CS1573</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="../README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="/" />

    <!-- The compilation internals are internal. The tests are the one thing that reads them, and
         they are asserting how a translation is produced rather than how it is consumed. -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="CupriLex.Harness.Tests" />

    <!--
      The survey tool, which is this repository's own and not a consumer. `shapes` and `reach`
      report on how the compiler reads a corpus - which call sites it reaches, what the corpus
      writes - and that is a question about the internals by definition. Answering it through a
      public API would mean publishing one for it.
    -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="cuprilex" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      A real JavaScript parser, and NO JavaScript engine. The distinction is the whole design:
      every tween is read out of the syntax tree and resolved by arithmetic, because a block that
      cannot be resolved must be refused rather than approximated, and an engine would happily
      approximate. Acornima is the maintained successor to Esprima.NET and is what Jint parses
      with, so it tracks the language rather than a snapshot of it.

      AngleSharp finds the inline scripts, pinned to the version CupriFace already resolves, so a
      document is read by one HTML parser on both sides of the comparison.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Acornima" Version="1.8.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="AngleSharp" Version="1.7.0" />

    <!--
      The CSS side of the same parser, for ONE question: what does the stylesheet already say this
      element's opacity and transform are? A tween to opacity 1 on an element the stylesheet
      authors at 0 is a fade in a browser and a still here, and 112 animations across 45 blocks
      were emitted flat for exactly that reason.

      DECLARED values, never computed ones. ComputeStyle() is the obvious call and it throws a
      NullReferenceException on `translate(-50%, -50%)`, the commonest centring idiom in this
      corpus - and a computed pixel value is the wrong answer anyway, because what a tween starts
      from is what the author wrote.

      Prerelease because that is the only form this package has ever had. Pinned exactly.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="AngleSharp.Css" Version="1.0.0-beta.154" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
